# Keeps the build context small and, more importantly, keeps host artefacts
# out of the images.
#
# backend/.venv and frontend/node_modules are the two that matter. Both are
# platform-specific — a Windows venv and Windows-resolved native modules —
# so copying them into a Linux image produces binaries that cannot run, on
# top of sending hundreds of megabytes to the daemon on every build. Each
# Dockerfile installs its own dependencies for its own platform.

# --- version control ---
.git
.gitignore
.github

# --- secrets ---
# The image must never bake in a key. Compose passes configuration as
# environment variables at run time instead.
.env
.env.local
*.pem

# --- Python ---
**/.venv
**/venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/*.egg-info
.model_cache

# --- Node ---
**/node_modules
**/.next
**/out
**/.turbo
**/npm-debug.log*
**/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# --- run artefacts ---
evaluation/reports/*.json
evaluation/reports/*.html
**/*.sqlite3
**/*.log

# --- editor / OS ---
.vscode
.idea
.claude
**/.DS_Store
**/Thumbs.db

# --- docs and compose itself ---
# Nothing in the image reads these; excluding them means a README edit does
# not invalidate the dependency layer.
README.md
docker-compose.yml
Makefile
